00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:00.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  london's waterloo station is one of  britain's biggest and busiest but not  today not this week three alternate  strike days on tuesday thursday and  saturday interspersed with three days of  minimal service mean travel misery for  millions this kind of disruption is just  ridiculous  everyone knows they're well paid how was  the journey  i'm coming from pakistan  over there i was supposed to take the  trend to  strafford it has been canceled four  times  from wimbledon it was fine but now going  to seven sisters is going to take me  very very long  so the journey will be one and a half  hours which is too much and everybody's  like messaged on the group work group  that there's heavy traffic on the roads  as well  so let's see here  only around 20  of the normal service is running and  only on half of the network mainly the  lines running into big cities in many  rural and regional areas there is no  train service at all  the dispute between the rmt rail union  and the employers network rail and the  train operating companies is largely  overpay and the impact of inflation on  workers wages but the government which  directly finances network rail has also  ordered it to find two and a half  billion dollars of savings network rail  says that's possible if staff accept new  working practices but the union says  that will mean 3 000 job losses and  jeopardize safety they want to rip up  our terms and conditions that we've  negotiated with the companies they're  even  threatening to lower pay and extend  working hours in the train operating  companies  and the majority of our members have not  had a pay increase for two to three  years so we've got a lot of problems we  will continue to do all we can to find  that sweet spot of compromise that is  affordable for for the employers  affordable for the taxpayer affordable  for the fair payer and the rmt and our  employers can live with at his regular  meeting of cabinet prime minister boris  johnson branded the strikes as  unnecessary and wrong he called on the  british public to stay the course during  what's expected to be a lengthy  disruption  the uk economy is experiencing the  combined effect of brexit the war in  ukraine and its effect on energy costs  as well as the inflationary pressure of  the post-pandemic recovery and rail  workers are not the only ones  complaining  the rmt is one of britain's strongest  unions some might say most militant of  unions with their ability to paralyze  the rail network during an industrial  dispute but the concerns that the rmt  have raised about the difference between  people's pay packets and the cost of  living at the moment the rate of  inflation are shared across many  different industries and other  professions teachers doctors nurses  local government workers all have pay  deals in the pipeline with the  government and they're likely to be  offered less than the rate of inflation  and that means a summer of discontent is  looming paul brennan al-jazeera waterloo  station  well the cost of living crisis has  triggered protests across the world in  the past few days from belgium to  zimbabwe the war in ukraine has pushed  up the price of food and fuel hitting  the poorest hard also making inflation  worse a sudden rise in demand for goods  as governments around the world opened  up after covet 19 lockdowns suppliers  were unable to ship products to  consumers fast enough and that's also  raised prices let's talk to given santa  who's live for us in london he's an  economist at king's college in london  good to have you with us so we know  what's causing the cost of living crisis  how long is it going to last how do we  solve it  well it's going to be at least this year  and next year is also going to be  difficult prices will still remain  elevated and we're looking at that  really damaging incomes across the globe  being pushed into hunger and destitution  here of course the uk a way of  industrial action that certainly could  be well repeated and on top of that  political instability as well the rising  price of making people worse off we  think 250 million more may be pushed  into extreme poverty this year and of  course governments toppled in pakistan  and sri lanka and the real key to  solving this is actually kind of a  two-pronged strategy on the one side  we'd finance ministries in rich  countries to give their citizens more  money to ensure they have enough cash to  get through this difficult moment more  international aid for those countries  who can't afford to give their citizens  money and central banks really to get  down inflation on their side by raising  interest rates as well as widening back  that quantitative easing program they  had that kind of accelerated throughout  the pandemic and given you you say 250  million people will be pushed into  extreme poverty where are those people  which countries are being worst affected  by this  well of course it's those nations that  are low-income nations particularly  especially those who have been kind of  liable or rather depend upon imported  wheat we know for example that global  food prices going higher it's really  going to hit the poorest people in the  poorest nations and sub-saharan africa  for example around 40 percent of  people's income is spent on food  compared to less than 20 in kind of rich  nations across the globe but as  generally speaking with this cost of  living crisis it's those right at the  bottom and in terms of nations those who  are the poorest nations what about  richer nations i mean  it's affecting  people everywhere isn't it i mean lots  of people are having to tighten their  belts and go without even basic staples  quite often  yeah that's certainly true in the uk we  already face a hunger crisis here 2.6  million kids going hungry we think  that's going to get worse again  specifically in the uk it is those who  are at the bottom and the lowest incomes  who are suffering the most and that's  because they spend more of their income  on food and on fuel to heat their homes  we think average inflation in the uk  will hit 11 percent later this year but  those are the lowest income states and  inflation rate of 15 a year so really  it's up for governments to kind of put  cash into families pockets but look  let's be clear rich nations did get  through a once in a century pandemic  they locked down their economies it's  certainly within their power to ensure  that no one suffers during this crisis  really good to talk to you given many  thanks indeed for being with us steven  sander there at king's college in london

